    "body": "## Summary\n\n`basis.int_to_state(state, bracket_notation=False)` returns an ambiguous, non-invertible string whenever `sps > 10` (i.e. any site can hold an occupation `>= 10`). The string cannot be parsed back by `state_to_int()` / `index()`, and within a fixed particle sector it is not even injective.\n\nThis affects both `basis_1d` (`boson_basis_1d`, high-`S` `spin_basis_1d`) and `basis_general`.\n\n## Reproduce\n\n```python\nfrom quspin.basis import boson_basis_1d\n\nbasis = boson_basis_1d(L=4, Nb=10)   # sps = Nb + 1 = 11 > 10\ns0 = basis.states[0]                 # the |10,0,0,0> state\n\nbasis.int_to_state(s0, bracket_notation=False)   # -> '10000'   (WRONG)\nbasis.state_to_int('10000')                       # -> ValueError: s must be representive state in basis.\n```\n\n`'10000'` is indistinguishable from a length-5 state `[1,0,0,0,0]`, and `list(map(int, '10000'))` mis-decodes it as `[1,0,0,0,0]`.\n\nThe representation is also **non-injective** inside a sector:\n\n```python\nbasis = boson_basis_1d(L=4, Nb=11)   # sps = 12\n# occupations (11,0,0,0) and (1,10,0,0) BOTH render as '11000'\n```\n\n## Root cause\n\nIn `basis_1d._int_to_state` / `basis_general._int_to_state` the non-bracket branch formats each occupation as a fixed-width **decimal** field and then strips all spaces:\n\n```python\nn_space = len(str(self.sps))\ns_str = \" \".join((\"{:\" + str(n_space) + \"d}\").format(bit) for bit in bits)\n...\nreturn s_str.replace(\" \", \"\")        # field boundaries destroyed for n_space > 1\n```\n\nBut `state_to_int()` / `index()` parse the string as a **base-`sps`** number via `int(s, self.sps)` (single-character digits `0-9`, then `a-z`). So for `sps > 10` the encoder (decimal, multi-char) and the decoder (base-`sps`, single-char) disagree, and there is no value of the string that both produce and accept.\n\nSeparately, `basis_1d._index` calls `int(s)` *before* checking `type(s) is str`, so a legitimate base-`sps` string containing letter digits (e.g. `'A000'` = occupation 10 in base 11) raises `ValueError: invalid literal for int() with base 10` before the string branch is reached.\n\n## Proposed fix\n\nMake the non-bracket string a genuine base-`sps` digit string — the exact inverse of `int(s, sps)`:\n\n```python\nreturn _np.base_repr(int(state), self.sps).zfill(self.N)\n```\n\n- `sps <= 10`: output is identical to before (`'2000'`, `'110'`, …).\n- `sps > 10`: occupation 10 becomes `'A'`, 11 `'B'`, … (base-`sps` digits), so the string round-trips through `state_to_int()` / `index()` and stays injective.\n- `sps > 36`: raise a clear error (beyond the base-36 digit alphabet that `int(s, base)` supports anyway).\n\nAlso reorder `basis_1d._index` to test `type(s) is str` before calling `int(s)`.\n\nA PR with this fix and a regression test (`test/test_int_to_state_multidigit.py`, which fails before and passes after; `sps <= 10` behaviour verified unchanged) follows.\n",
